>
  <h1>How due dates are calculated</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A due date is not a prediction of the day the baby arrives. It is a date counted forward
    from the first day of the last period, using a rule from the 1800s that clinics still use
    first. This page explains the rule, works two examples by hand, and says where it goes wrong.
  </p>
  <nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page"><p class="onpage-title">On this page</p><ul><li><a href="#the-short-answer">The short answer</a></li><li><a href="#the-rule-step-by-step">The rule, step by step</a></li><li><a href="#one-example-worked-by-hand">One example, worked by hand</a></li><li><a href="#why-count-from-the-last-period">Why count from the last period</a></li><li><a href="#when-the-rule-goes-wrong">When the rule goes wrong</a></li><li><a href="#questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</a></li></ul></nav>

  <h2 id="the-short-answer">The short answer</h2>
  <p>
    The standard method is Naegele’s rule, named after Franz Karl Naegele, a German obstetrician
    who published it in the early 1800s. Take the first day of the last menstrual period and add
    280 days. That is 40 weeks. The rule assumes a regular 28 day cycle with ovulation on day 14.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only about 1 baby in 20 arrives on the exact date. Most arrive within two weeks either side
    of it. The date is a planning date: it sets the schedule for scans, appointments and leave,
    not the day the birth will happen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-rule-step-by-step">The rule, step by step</h2>
  <p>
    The version most people are taught uses calendar months instead of counting days:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Start with the first day of the last period.</li>
    <li>Add 7 days.</li>
    <li>Subtract 3 months.</li>
    <li>Add 1 year.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The result is the estimated due date. Clinics usually count 280 days instead, and the two
    usually land on the same day or within a few days of each other. They can differ by a day or
    two because calendar months are uneven: a date counted through February lands differently
    from one counted through a 31 day month. For example, a last period starting 10 May 2026
    gives 17 February 2027 by the three step version and 14 February 2027 by the 280 day count.
    Both are estimates, and the gap does not matter, because the date is a planning date.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the cycle is longer or shorter than 28 days, shift the date by the difference. A 35 day
    cycle moves the date 7 days later, and a 21 day cycle moves it 7 days earlier, because
    ovulation usually moves with the cycle.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-example-worked-by-hand">One example, worked by hand</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Working</th><th scope="col">Date</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Last period starts</td><td>The date you enter</td><td class="num">1 Feb 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Add 7 days</td><td>1 Feb plus 7 days</td><td class="num">8 Feb 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Subtract 3 months</td><td>8 Feb minus 3 months</td><td class="num">8 Nov 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Add 1 year</td><td>8 Nov 2025 plus 1 year</td><td class="num">8 Nov 2026</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>Estimated due date</td><td>Same as adding 280 days</td><td class="num">8 Nov 2026</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Second example: with a last period starting 1 February 2026 and a 35 day cycle, the 280 day
    count plus the 7 day shift gives 15 November 2026. If the conception date is known instead,
    count 266 days from it: conception on 1 March 2026 gives 22 November 2026.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-count-from-the-last-period">Why count from the last period</h2>
  <p>
    Most people know the first day of their last period and do not know the day they conceived.
    Pregnancy is dated from the period, so the count starts about two weeks before conception in
    the standard model. That is why someone is called four weeks pregnant on the day a period is
    missed, and two weeks pregnant at conception. French practice counts the same span as
    semaines d’aménorrhée, weeks since the last period, and the arithmetic is the same.
  </p>

  <h2 id="when-the-rule-goes-wrong">When the rule goes wrong</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is a starting point, and it is wrong more often than people expect. The common
    cases:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Irregular cycles.</strong> The rule assumes ovulation on day 14. When cycles vary
      by more than a few days, the error grows, and the date is little more than a rough guide.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Recent hormonal contraception or breastfeeding.</strong> Cycles may not have
      settled into a regular pattern yet, so the last period is a weak anchor.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>IVF or a known conception date.</strong> Count 266 days from conception. For IVF,
      clinics count from the transfer date and the age of the embryo, and their date is the one
      to use.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The dating scan.</strong> An ultrasound in the first trimester, measuring the baby
      from head to bottom, is more accurate than any date counted from a period. If the scan date
      differs from the period date by more than about five to seven days, clinics use the scan
      date.
    </li>
  </ul>

