  <h2 id="questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>How accurate is a due date from my last period?</dt>
    <dd>
      Within about a week for most people with regular cycles, wider if the cycle varies by more
      than a few days or hormonal contraception was stopped recently. The dating scan is more
      accurate, which is why clinics use the scan date when the two disagree by more than a few
      days.
    </dd>
    <dt>Why 280 days and not 266?</dt>
    <dd>
      280 days counts from the first day of the last period, and 266 days counts from conception.
      The 14 days between them are the standard gap between the period and ovulation in a 28 day
      cycle. Both counts aim at the same date.
    </dd>
    <dt>What if my cycle is not 28 days?</dt>
    <dd>
      Shift the date by the difference between your cycle and 28 days. A 35 day cycle moves the
      date a week later, and a 21 day cycle moves it a week earlier. If the cycle is irregular,
      no shift fixes it, and the scan matters more.
    </dd>
    <dt>What if I know the conception date?</dt>
    <dd>
      Add 266 days to it. That is 280 days minus the 14 days Naegele’s rule assumes between the
      period and ovulation.
    </dd>
    <dt>Does the rule work after IVF?</dt>
    <dd>
      Not as written. Clinics date the pregnancy from the embryo transfer date and the age of the
      embryo at transfer. Use the date the clinic gives you.
    </dd>
    <dt>What does EDD mean?</dt>
    <dd>
      Estimated date of delivery, also called the estimated due date. The word estimated is doing
      real work: only about 1 baby in 20 arrives on that exact day.
    </dd>
  </dl>

